In a #heavy-bottomed saucepan{} over medium heat, melt the @unsalted butter{4%tbsp}.
Add the @onion{1/2%medium}(finely diced), @celery{1%stalk}(finely diced),
and @carrot{1}(finely diced).
Cook, stirring occasionally, until the vegetables are softened, ~{5-6%minutes}.

Add the @all-purpose flour{1/4%cup} to the pan with the vegetables and whisk to incorporate fully.
Reduce the heat to low and cook, whisking often, until the flour turns deep golden brown,
~{6-10%minutes}.

Gradually pour in the @brown beef stock{4%cups}(low-sodium or unsalted),
preferably home-made, and whisk simultaneously until there are no lumps in the sauce.
Stir in the @tomato puree{1/4%cup}, @salt{pinch}, @whole peppercorns{8}, @bay leaf{1},
and @?parsley stems{4}.

Bring the sauce to a boil over medium-high heat.
Adjust the heat to maintain a steady simmer (small bubbles occasionally rise to the
surface of the sauce).
Cook for ~{35-45%minutes}, stirring frequently to keep the bottom of the pot from scorching,
until the volume reduces by about 1/3.
When ready, the sauce should be just thick enough to coat the back of a spoon.
It is not a thick sauce, but approximately the consistency of heavy cream or thin gravy.

Line a large strainer with cheesecloth and set it over a bowl.
Strain the sauce into the bowl and discard the solids.

Store in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 5 days.
Reheat over low heat, whisking constantly, on the stovetop.
